## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle by requiring a lock-free hash map with per-bucket sequence counters to prevent ABA across NUMA boundaries, focusing on real-world inter-thread communication patterns.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free hashing with per-bucket sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Hazard pointers or epoch-based reclamation for safe node reclaim
- Cache-line alignment and padding to mitigate false sharing
- Linear probing vs open addressing trade-offs in bounded maps
- Correctness under wrap-around and visibility across NUMA domains

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  uint64_t seq;
  uint64_t key;
  uint64_t val;
} bucket_t;

bucket_t *map; // array of size 1<<16
size_t N = 1<<16;
```

```c
bool map_insert(bucket_t *b, uint64_t k, uint64_t v) {
  uint64_t s;
  for (;;) {
    s = atomic_load_explicit(&b->seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if ((s & 1) == 0) { // empty slot assumed
      if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&b->seq, &s, s + 1)) {
        b->key = k;
        b->val = v;
        atomic_store_explicit(&b->seq, s + 2, memory_order_release);
        return true;
      }
    } else if (b->key == k) {
      atomic_store_explicit(&b->val, v, memory_order_release);
      return true;
    } else {
      // probe next
    }
  }
}
```

